1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:07,450
One GPU. 24 GB. Your model needs 22. The last 2 sit empty.

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:13,850
Slicing can't help. Slices come in fixed sizes, and none leaves a 2 GB corner.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,060
So the small model gets a GPU of its own.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,360
Sardeenz doesn't slice.

5
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:29,200
It keeps a memory ledger, and packs each GPU by size.

6
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:37,770
Generative, predictive, embeddings. Different engines, different sizes, same GPU.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,040
One proxy routes every request to the right model.

8
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,750
More models than memory? The idle models sleep in host RAM.

9
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:58,150
A request for a sleeping model is parked, while the least recently used one is swapped out.

10
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:01,560
The client just sees a slow first token.

11
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:05,850
To Kubernetes, nothing moved.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,050
Sardeenz. Every gigabyte, working.

